/*  Snake. Arrow keys steer, `q` gives up, and the wall is unforgiving.

    ★★ ONE CODE PATH, playable and deterministic at the same time — there is no "demo mode" below
    and no `if anybody is watching`. Steering is read only when a key is ALREADY waiting, so an
    unattended run is simply a game nobody steers: the snake goes right, eats what is in its way,
    hits the wall, and the program ends. Every corpus run prints the same frames, and a person at
    a keyboard plays the real thing.

    ⚠⚠ MEASURED, and it is what makes that work: with input closed, POSIX `select` reports stdin
    READABLE and `read-key` answers -1 at once, while Windows `_kbhit` answers 0. The two
    platforms disagree about whether a key is waiting and agree about what is typed, which is
    nothing — so the loop stops LISTENING at -1 rather than treating it as a keypress, and both
    play the identical unsteered game.

    ★ The five files around this one are the whole of the structure: `board` the field, `serpent`
    the snake, `feeding` the apples, `screen` the picture, `steering` what a key means, `pacing`
    the clock. Nothing is pulled and nothing is a book — they share a directory, and a directory
    is one namespace.

    ★★ IT DRAWS OVER ITSELF. `terminals's home` puts the cursor back at the top left and the next
    frame is written on top of the last one — which works only because every frame is the SAME
    SHAPE, so the score line is padded to the width of the field rather than left to shrink from
    `score 10` to `score 9` and leave the `0` behind. That is the whole trick, and the one rule
    that comes with it.

    ⚠ The escape sequences land in the corpus output too, which is deliberate: they are the same
    bytes on both backends, so the oracle still means something, and the alternative was a second
    output path that nobody would ever look at. ⚠⚠ They travel on `State` lines rather than
    through `terminals's put` — an axiom's writes go straight to the process's stdout and never
    reach the interpreter's own output, so a frame drawn with `put` is invisible to half the
    oracle. Measured the hard way, by this file failing the corpus.
*/

Define snake as cast starting.
Define eaten as 0.
Define apple as cast apple-for on (snake, eaten).

/* Raw mode is asked for, never assumed — a pipe, a redirect or a machine without termios all
   say no, and the answer is believed. Without it the terminal collects a whole LINE before
   handing anything over, which is not a thing a game can wait for. */
Define raw as false.
If cast terminals's interactive, the raw becomes cast terminals's raw-on.

/*  Cursor sequences on, cursor out of the way, and one erase before the first frame. The erase
    happens ONCE: doing it per frame is what makes a terminal game flicker, because the screen is
    briefly empty between the wipe and the redraw. */
/*  The console is told to act on sequences; nothing is printed here. The erase and the hidden
    cursor ride on the FIRST frame's first line instead — one write, and no half-drawn screen
    between wiping and drawing. */
Cast terminals's screen-on.
Define opening as "{cast terminals's cursor-hidden}{cast terminals's cleared}".

/* Whether there is any point asking for a key. It goes false at end-of-input and stays there. */
Define listening as true.
Define gave-up as false.

Repeat:
    /*  Padded to the width of the field, so a shorter score cannot leave the last one's digits
        sitting behind it. Every line of the frame is exactly as wide as every other, which is
        what lets `home` replace an erase. */
    Define shown as "score {eaten}".
    While the length of shown is less than cast width, repeat:
        The shown becomes shown joined to " ".
    Done.

    /*  ★ The cursor is sent home on the FRONT of the score line rather than by a write of its
        own — one write per line, and `State` is what carries it. That matters beyond flicker:
        `State` goes through the interpreter's own output, and `terminals's put` does not, so a
        frame drawn with `put` would be invisible to the oracle on the interpreted side. */
    State "{opening}{cast terminals's home}{shown}".
    The opening becomes "".

    For each line in cast frame-of on (snake's body, apple), repeat:
        State line.
    Done.

    /*  The tick, in SLICES, with a look at the keyboard between each one. Waiting the whole tick
        and then reading would notice a keypress up to a tick late, which at this speed is the
        difference between a game that answers and one that argues.

        ⚠ `key-waiting` first, always. `read-key` WAITS — raw mode sets VMIN to 1 — so asking it
        without checking would stop the game until somebody pressed something, which is exactly
        the gap this pair of members was added to close. */
    Define pending as 0.
    Define waited as 0.
    While waited is less than 9, repeat:
        Cast nap on (10).

        While listening and cast terminals's key-waiting, repeat:
            Define tapped as cast terminals's read-key.
            If tapped is input-ended, the listening becomes false.
            Otherwise, if cast quits on (tapped), the gave-up becomes true.
            Otherwise, the pending becomes tapped.
        Done.

        Increment waited by 1.
    Done.

    If gave-up, stop.

    /*  ★★ ONE TURN PER TICK, decided against the direction the snake is STILL heading — and this
        is correctness, not tidiness. `turned` refuses to reverse onto the neck, but it can only
        judge the turn in front of it: heading right, an `up` and then a `left` inside one tick
        are each perfectly legal, and together they point the snake straight back down its own
        body. Keeping only the LAST arrow and judging it against the pre-move direction makes
        that pair a reversal again, which is what `turned` already knows how to refuse.

        ⚠ It only became reachable when the keyboard started being read nine times a tick instead
        of once. The faster you look, the more pairs fit between two moves. */
    The snake becomes cast steered on (snake, pending).

    /*  Eating is decided BEFORE the move, against the square the head is about to enter — the
        snake grows into the apple rather than onto it. Asking afterwards would have the tail
        already dropped, so the snake would eat and stay the same length. */
    Define feeding as cast apple's same-as on (cast snake's ahead).
    The snake becomes cast moved on (snake, feeding).

    If feeding:
        Increment eaten by 1.
        The apple becomes cast apple-for on (snake, eaten).
    Done.
Until not cast alive on (snake).

If raw, cast terminals's raw-off.

Define parting as cast terminals's cursor-shown.
If gave-up, state "{parting}You gave up on {eaten}.".
Otherwise, state "{parting}Game over. You ate {eaten}.".
